00:02:03.502 --> 00:02:05.854
Today I am covering A Nightmare on Elm Street.

00:02:05.902 --> 00:02:09.330
If there's any movie that needs no introduction, this is probably it.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.966
The film made 51 times its budget when it was released in the 80s,

00:02:12.988 --> 00:02:14.194
Put New Line Cinema on the map.

00:02:14.242 --> 00:02:16.501
They're now referred to as the House that Freddy built.

00:02:16.501 --> 00:02:18.975
Launched the career of actor Johnny Depp and took home

00:02:18.975 --> 00:02:23.462
two Saturn Awards in 1985 and was selected for preservation by the Library of Congress

00:02:23.526 --> 00:02:25.690
for Cultural Significance in 2021.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:27.654
I guess that is an introduction.

00:02:27.702 --> 00:02:31.874
But nearly 40 years later, there still seemed to be a litany of interpretations. 

00:02:31.874 --> 00:02:34.094
What is the film trying to say with its references to religion,

00:02:34.132 --> 00:02:38.707
literature and relationships? Who are surfers number one and number two?

00:02:38.707 --> 00:02:40.910
Let's cut in to the things you missed.

00:02:41.156 --> 00:02:43.240
(SLICE)

00:02:43.377 --> 00:02:47.219
🎵 80s Synth Music 🎵

00:02:47.219 --> 00:02:49.022
The film begins inside the dream of Tina.

00:02:49.086 --> 00:02:52.034
She's in a damp hallway and a lamb can be seen running across it.

00:02:52.072 --> 00:02:53.854
The first of the film's religious symbols.

00:02:53.902 --> 00:02:57.330
This is representative of the sacrificial lamb, a symbol in many major

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.867
religions where a lamb is killed in order to save others for the greater good.

00:03:00.867 --> 00:03:03.766
In Judaism, the lamb's blood is smeared on the doors of those who are

00:03:03.788 --> 00:03:06.178
to be passed over by the angel of death on Passover.

00:03:06.274 --> 00:03:10.034
In Islam, it's believed that God provided Abraham with a lamb to sacrifice

00:03:10.082 --> 00:03:12.218
in place of having to sacrifice his own son.

00:03:12.304 --> 00:03:16.730
In Christianity, the sacrifice of Jesus is known as the Lamb of God,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:17.978
not that Lamb of God.

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:21.238
The image here indicates that Tina will be sacrificed in the name of helping

00:03:21.254 --> 00:03:24.198
another character, in this case Nancy Thompson, survive.

00:03:24.294 --> 00:03:27.322
After waking up, Tina grabs her crucifix off the wall for protection,

00:03:27.386 --> 00:03:30.078
essentially equating Freddy Krueger with the devil, a theme that would

00:03:30.084 --> 00:03:32.510
persist through this movie and notably, one other.

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.566
When Nancy sleeps over the next night to offer Tina some comfort, 

00:03:35.588 --> 00:03:36.734
the crucifix falls on her.

00:03:36.772 --> 00:03:39.858
Later, we see the ceiling warp above her as Freddy tries to cross the

00:03:39.864 --> 00:03:41.282
threshold into the real world.

00:03:41.336 --> 00:03:44.750
But then he retracts, probably after seeing Nancy with the crucifix.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:48.302
By 1984, when A Nightmare on Elm Street came out, the trope of the

00:03:48.366 --> 00:03:51.398
virgin Final Girl had already been established with the likes of

00:03:51.404 --> 00:03:52.066
Laurie Strode and Alice Hardy.

00:03:52.098 --> 00:03:55.138
But it's also important to examine the reason that the trope exists.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.598
It was a morality play.

00:03:56.684 --> 00:04:00.106
Early slashers were the evolution of campfire stories warning kids about

00:04:00.128 --> 00:04:01.398
improper behavior.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:02.554
-[Stu] That's right.

00:04:02.672 --> 00:04:06.694
-[Stu] Now you're no longer a virgin, now you gotta die.

00:04:06.822 --> 00:04:07.910
-[Stu] Those are the rules.

00:04:07.990 --> 00:04:11.834
But where did the idea that sex is supposedly immoral come from in the first place?

00:04:11.872 --> 00:04:15.674
Primarily from Christianity, whose Bible only details its founders

00:04:15.722 --> 00:04:17.658
losing their virginity after marriage.

00:04:17.754 --> 00:04:21.246
In this case, Tina and her boyfriend Rod get it on in her parents' room, 

00:04:21.268 --> 00:04:24.654
in stark contrast to Nancy and her boyfriend Glen, who follow the rules by

00:04:24.692 --> 00:04:27.494
sleeping in separate rooms entirely, much to Glen's dismay.

00:04:27.562 --> 00:04:29.566
As a result, Tina is the first to die. And when she comes face to face with Freddy,

00:04:29.598 --> 00:04:34.537
she begs for forgiveness from her maker for breaking rule number one.

00:04:34.696 --> 00:04:38.738
- [Tina] Please God. 
- [Freddy] This... is God. 

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:42.626
Her pleas are not answered, however, but by the end of the movie, neither

00:04:42.658 --> 00:04:43.714
are Nancy's.

00:04:43.842 --> 00:04:48.342
-[Nancy] And now I lay me down to sleep, I pray the Lord my soul to keep.

00:04:48.476 --> 00:04:54.154
-[Nancy] And if I die before I wake, I pray the Lord my soul to take.

00:04:54.272 --> 00:04:57.814
When she goes into the final dream to face Freddy, she finds Tina's crucifix

00:04:57.862 --> 00:04:59.034
and takes it along with her.

00:04:59.072 --> 00:05:00.042
But it doesn't help her.

00:05:00.096 --> 00:05:03.722
It is actually her refusal to believe in Freddy that ultimately defeats him.

00:05:03.776 --> 00:05:07.082
It certainly flies in the face of movies like The Exorcist or The Conjuring,

00:05:07.146 --> 00:05:09.642
in which the character's faith helps them overcome the evil.

00:05:09.706 --> 00:05:11.738
In Nancy's case, it's quite the opposite.

00:05:11.834 --> 00:05:15.786
In an interview in Fangoria magazine for his 2010 film My Soul to Take,

00:05:15.818 --> 00:05:19.154
Wes Craven confirms that he is an atheist, saying that if there is a God,

00:05:19.192 --> 00:05:21.794
He doesn't really intervene in the horrible things that happen on Earth very

00:05:21.832 --> 00:05:22.402
much at all.

00:05:22.456 --> 00:05:24.654
So we kind of have to embody it all ourselves.

00:05:24.782 --> 00:05:26.770
And I think that's what Nancy is doing here.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.942
She doesn't receive divine intervention, and her parents are no help either.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:32.278
So she decides to take matters into her own hands.

00:05:32.364 --> 00:05:36.134
Unlike many final girls and horror protagonists, she proactively goes after

00:05:36.172 --> 00:05:39.590
her stalker, not just trying to survive, but trying to put an end to it.

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.886
In the final dream sequence, she descends into the boiler room,

00:05:42.918 --> 00:05:44.730
Freddy's domain, which seems to represent hell.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.946
She goes down this spiral staircase, but when she encounters Freddy,

00:05:47.968 --> 00:05:51.798
She flees and goes down the same spiral staircase again, suggesting that she's

00:05:51.814 --> 00:05:54.022
in an infinite loop or eternal damnation.

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:57.246
However, she's able to break the loop and inexplicably lands outside her house,

00:05:57.268 --> 00:06:00.126
showing that the nightmare will follow her wherever she goes.

00:06:00.228 --> 00:06:03.294
When audiences first saw A Nightmare on Elm Street, Tina was the first

00:06:03.332 --> 00:06:06.174
character the film followed, so people assumed that she was the main

00:06:06.212 --> 00:06:08.110
character until her early departure.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.486
This is an homage to Marion Crane, the first girl that we follow in

00:06:11.518 --> 00:06:14.766
Alfred Hitchcock's Psycho, that movie that gets brought up in nearly every episode

00:06:14.798 --> 00:06:17.426
of Things You Missed because every horror filmmaker wants to pay their

00:06:17.448 --> 00:06:18.910
respects to one of the greats.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.434
Even her hair resembles the way that Janet Leigh's hair was styled in Psycho.

00:06:22.482 --> 00:06:25.766
Tina's actress, Amanda Wyss has confirmed that her character was a nod to Hitchcock.

00:06:25.788 --> 00:06:29.058
There's another horror movie reference later on when Nancy tries

00:06:29.074 --> 00:06:31.826
to keep herself awake by watching The Evil Dead on TV.

00:06:31.938 --> 00:06:35.786
This is actually a response by director Wes Craven because Sam Raimi, the

00:06:35.808 --> 00:06:39.018
director of The Evil Dead, put a poster for Craven's The Hills Have Eyes in

00:06:39.024 --> 00:06:40.006
the basement of the cabin.

00:06:40.038 --> 00:06:40.822
In The Evil Dead.

00:06:40.886 --> 00:06:43.974
This would set off a chain of events of the two directors cross referencing

00:06:44.022 --> 00:06:47.086
each other's work, much like Craven's back and forth with Kevin Smith during

00:06:47.108 --> 00:06:47.978
the Scream era.

00:06:48.074 --> 00:06:51.774
And on the topic of people named Kevin, some of you may have seen this

