00:07:40.838 --> 00:07:43.784
🎵 Creepy Music 🎵

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:47.566
When I saw the missing poster for Kevin Collins, my first instinct was to

00:07:47.588 --> 00:07:50.334
check the credits, thinking that it might be a crew reference Easter egg.

00:07:50.382 --> 00:07:53.842
What I found was that this was actually a real missing kid in 1984.

00:07:53.896 --> 00:07:56.946
And although this poster was featured in both A Nightmare on Elm Street and

00:07:56.968 --> 00:08:01.718
the Terminator. Kevin was sadly never found. Despite that one being a dud,

00:08:01.724 --> 00:08:05.042
I was still able to find a few crew reference Easter eggs,

00:08:05.106 --> 00:08:06.322
or at least crew adjacent Easter eggs.

00:08:06.386 --> 00:08:09.538
Nancy's English teacher is played by Lin Shaye, the sister of the movie's

00:08:09.554 --> 00:08:10.770
producer, Robert Shaye.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:13.526
Yes, it's the same Lin Shaye who went on to be an Insidious.

00:08:13.638 --> 00:08:17.050
Robert Shaye's daughter is also referenced. Due to Nancy's sleep issues,

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:20.586
Her mother takes her to a sleep clinic called the Katyj Institute, which is

00:08:20.608 --> 00:08:22.070
named after Katja Shaye.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:26.074
Having a producer for a dad has its perks, as does marrying the director.

00:08:26.122 --> 00:08:29.770
The nurse at the institute is played by Wes Craven's then wife, Mimi Craven.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:33.246
The in universe name of this character is Dorrie Cooper, which is an alternate

00:08:33.278 --> 00:08:38.070
spelling of Dorfee Cooper, the film's set dresser. The sleep institute, however, is not much help.

00:08:38.632 --> 00:08:41.566
-[Doctor] She's into REM now. 

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:47.554
Later on, there's also a cop named Huntley, likely named after the set

00:08:47.592 --> 00:08:48.974
decorator, Anne Huntley.

00:08:49.022 --> 00:08:52.134
Throughout the movie, Nancy struggles with this thin line between being

00:08:52.172 --> 00:08:53.522
awake and being asleep.

00:08:53.586 --> 00:08:56.854
Every time she begins to doze off, Freddy attempts to cross over into her world.

00:08:56.892 --> 00:09:00.274
At the end of the movie, that line between sleep and awake is blurred

00:09:00.322 --> 00:09:03.078
when she thinks that she's woken up from a nightmare only to find that she's

00:09:03.094 --> 00:09:04.470
still in Freddy's domain.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.558
The dreamverse reality theme is harped on in her English class while they're

00:09:07.574 --> 00:09:08.726
learning about Shakespeare.

00:09:08.838 --> 00:09:11.710
-[Teacher] What is seen is not always what is real.

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:17.034
-[Teacher]According to Shakespeare, there was something operating in nature,

00:09:17.082 --> 00:09:19.642
-[Teacher] perhaps, inside human nature itself, that was rotten.

00:09:19.786 --> 00:09:23.182
Also written on the chalkboard is a quote from Cinna, the poet from the play

00:09:23.236 --> 00:09:24.202
Julius Caesar.

00:09:24.266 --> 00:09:27.726
I dreamt tonight that I did feast with Caesar, and things unluckily charge

00:09:27.758 --> 00:09:28.542
my fantasy.

00:09:28.606 --> 00:09:32.350
I have no will to wander forth of doors, yet something leads me forth.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:35.666
Cinna is telling about a dream where his feast with Caesar turned dark and

00:09:35.688 --> 00:09:38.958
ominous, a foreshadowing of the character's death, not unlike the nightmares

00:09:38.974 --> 00:09:41.762
that Rod and Tina both experienced before their own deaths.

00:09:41.826 --> 00:09:45.014
As Nancy toes the line between dream and reality, there's one other

00:09:45.052 --> 00:09:47.954
character who mirrors her struggle, and that is her mom, Marge Thompson.

00:09:48.002 --> 00:09:51.594
Marge lives in a constant limbo between being sober and being drunk,

00:09:51.632 --> 00:09:54.922
constantly accessing bottles of gin hidden around the house.

00:09:55.056 --> 00:09:58.826
Marge and Nancy's father, Donald, seem to be divorced, and it's not hard to

00:09:58.848 --> 00:10:01.926
imagine that the aftermath of their participation of the lynching of

00:10:01.958 --> 00:10:03.482
Freddy Krueger had something to do with it.

00:10:03.536 --> 00:10:06.638
The alcoholism and the divorce have put a strain on Marge's relationship

00:10:06.724 --> 00:10:07.402
with her daughter.

00:10:07.466 --> 00:10:10.974
Nancy hides most of her life from her mom and is scornful in what she does share.

00:10:11.012 --> 00:10:14.206
She sneaks her boyfriend in through the window, lies about her

00:10:14.228 --> 00:10:17.538
whereabouts during Tina's death, does what she can to hide her sleep issues,

00:10:17.624 --> 00:10:20.734
including hiding a secret coffee machine under her bed to get some caffeine

00:10:20.782 --> 00:10:21.918
under her mom's nose.

00:10:22.014 --> 00:10:25.486
Marge is constantly trying to get Nancy to sleep, but she's obviously afraid

00:10:25.518 --> 00:10:26.178
to do so.

00:10:26.264 --> 00:10:29.430
-[Marge] You are going to get some sleep tonight if it kills me.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:33.026
The irony is not lost here, although this would change as later sequels

00:10:33.058 --> 00:10:34.162
added to the Freddy lore.

00:10:34.226 --> 00:10:37.478
In this scenario, Freddy Krueger represents the damage caused by an

00:10:37.484 --> 00:10:38.534
alcoholic parent.

00:10:38.652 --> 00:10:41.814
Throughout the movie, we see her rebelling and shutting her mom out in order

00:10:41.852 --> 00:10:43.246
to protect herself against Freddy.

00:10:43.298 --> 00:10:46.166
Their relationship is summed up perfectly at the Sleep Disorder Institute

00:10:46.198 --> 00:10:48.170
when Marge begs for her daughter's trust.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:53.514
-[Nancy] It's not you I don't trust, it's just

00:10:53.552 --> 00:10:54.058
She can't even think of anything else to say.

00:10:54.144 --> 00:10:57.566
Nancy later calls her mother out for using drinking to numb her feelings, to

00:10:57.588 --> 00:11:00.570
which she gets slapped in the face, a clear cut sign of abuse.

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:03.886
Kids who have dealt with fighting parents or abuse often times find that it

00:11:03.908 --> 00:11:06.902
manifests in their own adult relationships with feelings of shame.

00:11:06.986 --> 00:11:09.986
They try to hide their own desires because they've seen the turmoil that it

00:11:10.008 --> 00:11:13.650
caused their own families and associate those desires with feelings of pain.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:17.054
This is illustrated in Nancy's reluctance with her relationship with Glen,

00:11:17.102 --> 00:11:18.814
but also in the bathtub scene.

00:11:18.862 --> 00:11:22.018
She's locked herself away in the bathroom, and she's dozing off in the tub,

00:11:22.114 --> 00:11:25.638
thinking about who knows what when Fredy reaches his clawed hand up out of

00:11:25.644 --> 00:11:29.266
the water between her legs, very much representative of the desires she's

00:11:29.298 --> 00:11:30.226
trying to repress.

00:11:30.338 --> 00:11:33.514
When her mom knocks on the door and wakes her up, freddy is suddenly sent

00:11:33.552 --> 00:11:35.718
away, indicative of Nancy's shame.

00:11:35.814 --> 00:11:39.206
But she doses off again, which leads to her getting pulled under by Freddy

00:11:39.238 --> 00:11:40.774
and nearly drowning in her dream.

00:11:40.822 --> 00:11:43.966
When her mom comes into the bathroom to help, nancy is screaming at her and

00:11:43.988 --> 00:11:45.070
claims nothing is wrong.

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:48.750
It seems like strange behavior because there is clearly something very wrong

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:52.266
every time she falls asleep, but her damaged parental relationship causes

00:11:52.298 --> 00:11:53.726
her to hide these issues away.

00:11:53.828 --> 00:11:57.226
On the topic of adult relationship metaphors, there's one scene where Nancy

00:11:57.258 --> 00:12:00.258
goes into her dream to find Freddy, with Glen keeping watch over her to

00:12:00.264 --> 00:12:01.694
wake her up if she runs into trouble.

00:12:01.742 --> 00:12:05.502
Glen is the one person she feels she trusts to do this, but he falls asleep

00:12:05.566 --> 00:12:08.738
and Fredy finds her and chases her back to her bedroom where they engage in

00:12:08.744 --> 00:12:12.146
a physical confrontation which involves, I'll just say, a lot of rolling

00:12:12.178 --> 00:12:15.510
around on the bed as Glen is blissfully unaware a few feet away.

00:12:15.580 --> 00:12:18.402
Once again, this represents Nancy fighting her inner demons.

00:12:18.466 --> 00:12:21.998
She secretly craves intimacy but fears ending up in a relationship that'll

00:12:22.034 --> 00:12:23.610
go south like her parents did.

00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:26.998
She comes home one day to find her house completely locked down with metal

00:12:27.014 --> 00:12:28.070
bars over the windows.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:31.722
Her freedom and agency is literally being restricted, just as she often

00:12:31.776 --> 00:12:34.150
finds herself trapped in a dream with Freddy Krueger.

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:37.038
At one point through the bars, Nancy waves to her father, who is

00:12:37.044 --> 00:12:39.002
investigating Glen's death across the street.

00:12:39.066 --> 00:12:42.398
The scene is symbolic of what some kids go through during a divorce.

00:12:42.484 --> 00:12:45.886
A parent becoming overly territorial and shutting the child out from

00:12:45.908 --> 00:12:47.262
his or her other parent in fear of losing them.

00:12:47.316 --> 00:12:50.606
In an earlier scene, Rod is suspected of being the killer and Nancy's father

00:12:50.638 --> 00:12:53.538
has the police force keep an eye on her, which quickly helps them find and

00:12:53.544 --> 00:12:54.270
catch Rod.

00:12:54.350 --> 00:12:57.842
Nancy accuses her father of using her to get to Rod and this is another

00:12:57.896 --> 00:13:01.010
symptom of what sons or daughters might go through in a messy divorce.

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:04.550
This is known as putting kids in the middle and often consists of one parent

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.106
using their child for leverage in the divorce proceedings.

00:13:07.218 --> 00:13:10.486
As I mentioned, her parents are no help to her and Nancy tries to take

00:13:10.508 --> 00:13:11.974
matters into her own hands.

00:13:12.092 --> 00:13:14.682
In the final scene, she thinks she's defeated Freddy for good.

00:13:14.736 --> 00:13:18.026
She tells her mom that she loves her and they make up, but before long, she

00:13:18.048 --> 00:13:19.930
finds that she's trapped by Freddy once again.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.250
To me, this last scene hammers the point home.

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:25.886
Being a minor with an addict parent is a nightmare that you can't wake up from

00:13:25.908 --> 00:13:30.430
As for surfer number one and surfer number two in the credits,

00:13:30.500 --> 00:13:31.118
I have no idea.

00:13:31.204 --> 00:13:33.422
There's no surfers in the movie as far as I can tell.

00:13:33.476 --> 00:13:36.746
I know Robert England, who plays Freddy Krueger used to be a very active

00:13:36.778 --> 00:13:40.430
surfer and was in a surfing movie called Big Wednesday in 1978.

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:43.838
Maybe there's a deleted and lost scene where Freddy goes after some kids on

00:13:43.844 --> 00:13:44.570
a surfboard.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:46.566
It's sounds like something that actually wouldn't be

00:13:46.588 --> 00:13:50.082
That out of the question in one of the weird and wacky Elm Street sequels.

00:13:50.146 --> 00:13:53.654
Which, by the way, I'm planning on covering in future episodes of Things You Missed.

00:13:53.692 --> 00:13:56.306
You'll find those videos in the playlist on the left when they're released.

00:13:56.338 --> 00:13:59.686
So if you need a little more Freddy in your life, remember to

00:13:59.708 --> 00:14:02.422
subscribe to CZsWorld for new horrors every week.

00:14:02.476 --> 00:14:06.470
Ring the Deathbell for all notifications and I will see you in the next one,

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:08.290
assuming we both survive.

